{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794381795,"nonce":"hyK9bzTTx06FbLo/Y6jNDrPMrxw=","title":"Headless::new races on the global context init when two tests build one in a process","message":"`Headless::new` (blinc_recorder/src/testing/headless.rs) does `if !BlincContextState::is_initialized() { init_with_callback(..) }`. `BlincContextState::init*` panics with \"init() called more than once\" when the cell is already set, so two threads that both pass the check race, and the second panics. The same check-then-init shape guards `OverlayContext`.\n\nSeen in the russl toy (`toy/tests/render.rs`: two tests, each building a `Headless`) under plain `cargo test`, which runs both in one process on separate threads: 1 failure in 4 runs on Blinc 3f98dc53, 0 in 4 on 2ca5f509. `blinc_recorder` and `context_state.rs` are identical between the two, so the code did not change; the run counts are too small to say the rate did. With `--test-threads=1`, and under `cargo nextest` (one process per test, 3 of 3), both tests pass.\n\nBlinc's own CI runs nextest, which hides it. Anything that runs several Headless tests in one process, as a downstream crate's `cargo test` does, can hit it.\n\nDirection: make the init idempotent and race-free (a `Once`/`OnceLock`-based `init_if_unset`, or have `Headless::new` treat \"already set\" as success), for both the context state and the overlay context.","files":null}]}